---
/**
 * A small drawn flower, used as a pin at the corner of a photograph.
 *
 * It appears in exactly two places — the polaroid beside the dress code and the
 * Our Story photograph — and it lives in its own file rather than being written
 * out twice so that it stays one drawing. Two copies of a `d` attribute are two
 * different drawings the moment anybody edits one of them and not the other.
 *
 * This component draws and does nothing else. It owns the geometry, the stroke
 * and its own 22px box; it owns no position at all. Which corner it sits in, how
 * far it laps over the photograph's edge and what happens on hover are the host
 * section's business, because they differ in the two places and depend on things
 * only the host knows — a mount's 10px of shell on one, a bare 4:5 frame on the
 * other. Each host therefore wraps this in an absolutely positioned element of
 * its own, which has the second virtue of keeping that positioning inside the
 * host's own style scope instead of reaching across a component boundary to
 * style somebody else's root element.
 *
 * The drawing. Five petals are a single path: each is one cubic that leaves the
 * centre and comes straight back to it, so the whole head is an M and five Cs
 * and the loops are symmetric teardrops without a single coordinate being
 * guessed. A small circle sits over the point where all five pinch together — at
 * this size ten line ends meeting is a blot, and a drawn flower wants an eye
 * there anyway.
 *
 * The stem is one quadratic, and where it BEGINS is the only fussy number here.
 * Five petals spaced 72deg apart, drawn this plump, leave the centre at almost
 * exactly 36deg either side of their own axis — so the lobes fill the full turn
 * and there is no wedge at the centre for a stem to come out of. The two lower
 * petals' inner edges in fact run down the vertical axis for the first three
 * units, which is precisely where a stem drawn from the centre would go, and
 * three coincident hairlines is a smudge rather than a drawing. So the stem
 * starts at 11.4 instead — a third of a unit inside the head's lower silhouette,
 * which at a 1px stroke is touching it, so the join reads as a join and every
 * point below it is clear of every petal. Those first three units are then drawn
 * by the petals themselves, which is no loss: a flower has a thicker neck under
 * the head.
 *
 * Line only: no fill, 1px ochre, and `vector-effect: non-scaling-stroke` so the
 * weight holds however the box is scaled, which is the same idiom as Rings and
 * LineArt.
 *
 * `aria-hidden`, and both hosts place it out of flow, so it is in no reading
 * order, takes no pointer and reserves no space.
 */
---

<svg
  class="pin-flower"
  viewBox="0 0 24 24"
  width="22"
  height="22"
  fill="none"
  aria-hidden="true"
  focusable="false"
>
  <path
    class="pin-flower__petals"
    d="M12 8.6C18.2 0.2 5.8 0.2 12 8.6C21.91 11.9 18.07 0.11 12 8.6C11.92 19.04 21.95 11.75 12 8.6C2.05 11.75 12.08 19.04 12 8.6C5.93 0.11 2.1 11.9 12 8.6"
  ></path>
  <circle class="pin-flower__eye" cx="12" cy="8.6" r="1.5"></circle>
  <path class="pin-flower__stem" d="M12 11.4Q13.15 17.4 11.4 22.6"></path>
</svg>

<style>
  .pin-flower {
    display: block;
    /* Fixed rather than fluid. It is a mark of a particular size, not a picture
       that should grow with what it is pinned to — and a fixed box is one less
       thing that can be measured differently on two different photographs. */
    width: 22px;
    height: 22px;
  }

  .pin-flower path,
  .pin-flower circle {
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--color-ochre);
    /* Held at 1px whatever the box does, exactly as in Rings — the flower is
       drawn on a 24 unit grid and rendered at 22px, so without this the line
       would come out a hair under a pixel and go grey. */
    stroke-width: 1;
    stroke-linecap: round;
    stroke-linejoin: round;
    vector-effect: non-scaling-stroke;
  }
</style>
